{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Take, pray, your son, your only one, whom you love, Isaac.</b> Scripture lengthened the language of the charge in order to magnify the command. This is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: <b>'Take, pray, your son'</b> — which son? He said to him, <b>'your only one.'</b> This one is an only son to his mother and this one is an only son to his mother. He said to him, <b>'whom you love.'</b> Both of them I love. He said to him, <b>'Isaac.'</b>" "<b>And go for yourself.</b> Ten trials was Abraham tried and found whole in all of them. And the first was 'Go for yourself from your land,' and the matter of the binding was the last trial." "<b>To the land of Moriah.</b> Mount Moriah is Jerusalem, and the Holy One, blessed be He, called the whole land Moriah after Mount Moriah that is within it. And that it is called Jerusalem 'a mountain' is because it is from the expression 'I will go for myself to the mountain of myrrh (mor) and cinnamon (kinamon),' and good spices grew there. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: cinnamon grew in the land of Israel, and the goats and gazelles would feed on it. And Onkelos translated: 'a land of worship (pulḥana).' He derived it from mora (fear/awe), for there they are destined to stand in awe before HaShem and to serve before Him." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> Mount Moriah — from there instruction (hora'ah) went forth to the world." "<b>And bring him up there for a burnt offering.</b> It should have said 'and bring him up there a burnt offering (olah).' But by way of the plain sense (peshat), this lamed is explanatory in the sense of 'in place of,' meaning that you should bring him up there in place of a burnt offering. Like it (Genesis 6): הלבנה לאבן — 'for mortar in place of stone.' For if you interpret לעולה as an actual burnt offering, how could the Holy One, blessed be He, command and then afterward retract His command? And Abraham, from the intensity of his love, understood an actual burnt offering, while the Holy One, blessed be He, who came to test him, intended only 'in place of a burnt offering.' And this is what is written: 'which I did not command, nor did I speak, nor did it enter My heart.' Also, it is possible that it alludes to the secret of the sacrifice, and therefore He said לעולה — to bring him up there for the tenth measure that ascends. And this trial is the tenth, and similarly with the ram that was offered in his stead, this same expression occurs, for it did not say 'and he took the ram and brought it up as a burnt offering,' but rather 'and he brought it up לעולה.' And because of this he added and said 'in place of his son,' concerning whom it was said והעלהו שם לעולה. And so Isaac said, 'and where is the lamb לעולה?' and so his father answered him, 'Elohim will provide for Himself the lamb לעולה, my son.' And behold, the word לעולה is the altar that He will mention shortly with the definite article, and the discerning will understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:11.463829183Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}